Ahem. The way _I_ heard it (and I have heard it this way several times,
from different sources) is this:
You have a particle that has a 50% chance of decaying within a specified
time. A machine will detect this, and if it decays, will trigger a
switch that lets a hammer fall on a capsule of poison gas in a box with
a cat in it. (Exact details on method of felicide vary.)
The point is this: One way of making sense of certain of the weirder
results of quantum mechanics is to say that certain events do not
collapse into one state or another until they are observed. The
particle decaying is such an event: until you make some kind of
measurement, the particle is neither "decayed" nor "non-decayed",
but simply in a state of haveing a 50% chance of being either.
But, said Schroedinger, this is ridiculous, because quantum events can
have macroscopic consequences. In the case of the cat, you would have
to conclude that the cat is half alive and half dead, until you open
the box, at which point it either dies or doesn't die. This, says
Schroedinger, is absurd.
Whether you agree with Schroedinger or not, I will let you decide.
It strikes me that the cat is an observer.

